CAT = "rsracPlanning"; SEG = "PLN"
Q = [
("In a cooling load calculation, which gain is latent rather than sensible?",
 "Vapour from occupants", ["Sun through west windows", "Heat from lighting", "Conduction through the roof"],
 "Latent load is moisture that must be condensed out of the air. Solar, lighting and conduction gains raise dry-bulb temperature, which is sensible load."),
("A room is at 24 C dry bulb with a dew point of 13 C. A duct surface at 10 C passing through it will:",
 "Collect condensation", ["Stay dry", "Raise the room humidity", "Lower the room's dew point to 10 C"],
 "Any surface colder than the air's dew point condenses moisture. That is why cold ducts and suction lines in occupied spaces are insulated with a vapour barrier."),
("On a psychrometric chart, cooling air without removing moisture moves the state point:",
 "Horizontally to the left", ["Straight up", "Down along the wet-bulb line", "Right along the saturation curve"],
 "Sensible cooling lowers dry bulb with no change in moisture content, a horizontal move to the left. Once the coil is below dew point, the line also drops."),
("Why is a suction riser sized for higher gas velocity than a horizontal suction line?",
 "To carry oil up to the compressor", ["To lower the pressure drop", "To raise the discharge temperature", "To stop liquid flashing"],
 "Oil travels as a film pushed by vapour, and moving it upward needs enough velocity. A riser sized too large lets oil pool at the bottom at low load."),
("Where is a P-trap placed on a long suction riser from an evaporator below the compressor?",
 "At the bottom of the riser", ["At the top of the riser", "At the compressor inlet", "Halfway up the horizontal run"],
 "A trap at the base collects oil until it builds enough to be carried up as a slug, keeping oil moving back to the compressor."),
("What does a double suction riser allow on a system with wide capacity steps?",
 "Oil return at all loads", ["Two refrigerants in one circuit", "Faster pump-down", "Use of a smaller compressor"],
 "At low load the large riser fills with oil at its trap and only the small riser carries vapour fast enough to lift oil. At full load both carry flow."),
("Which rating compares cooling output to electrical input at one set of conditions?",
 "EER", ["SEER", "HSPF", "AFUE"],
 "EER is cooling output divided by power input at one rating point. SEER averages a cooling season, HSPF rates heat pump heating and AFUE rates fuel-burning furnaces."),
("A heat pump delivers 10.5 kW of heat while drawing 3 kW. What is its coefficient of performance?",
 "3.5", ["0.29", "7.5", "13.5"],
 "COP is heat delivered divided by energy input: 10.5 / 3 = 3.5. Dividing input by output gives 0.29, and adding or subtracting the figures gives the other values."),
("What is a heat pump's thermal balance point?",
 "Where output matches heat loss", ["Where defrost begins", "Point of equal suction and discharge pressure", "Setting where the fan stops"],
 "Below the balance point the building loses heat faster than the heat pump supplies it, so supplementary heat is needed. It guides sizing and backup heat staging."),
("Why should an air-cooled condenser not be placed tight in a corner between two walls?",
 "Hot discharge air recirculates", ["Walls block refrigerant flow", "Snow cannot melt there", "Noise is louder in the open"],
 "Without the maker's clearances, warm discharge air is drawn back in, raising head pressure and cutting capacity. Clearances are in the installation instructions."),
("Which code sets machinery room requirements for refrigerating systems in Canada?",
 "CSA B52", ["CSA B149.1", "CSA C22.1", "CSA B64"],
 "CSA B52, the Mechanical Refrigeration Code, covers refrigerant quantity limits, machinery rooms and relief devices. B149.1 is gas, C22.1 is electrical and B64 is backflow."),
("A refrigerant marked A2L in ASHRAE Standard 34 is:",
 "Lower toxicity, mildly flammable", ["Higher toxicity, non-flammable", "Lower toxicity, highly flammable", "Higher toxicity, mildly flammable"],
 "The letter gives toxicity (A lower, B higher) and the number flammability (1 none, 2L lower flammability, 2, 3 higher). A2L covers R-32 and R-454B."),
("Why does a refrigerant's safety class affect where an evaporator can be placed in an occupied space?",
 "Room size limits the allowed charge", ["Flammable units cannot be ducted", "A1 units need a machinery room", "Coil size sets the safety class"],
 "Codes limit how much refrigerant may leak into an occupied space by room volume and safety class, so flammable refrigerants face smaller charge limits or need mitigation."),
("Where should a room thermostat or temperature sensor be placed?",
 "Interior wall, away from heat", ["Above a supply air diffuser", "Beside a sunny south window", "On an outside wall by the door"],
 "A sensor on an interior wall at about chest height, away from sun, drafts and appliances, reads the room's real temperature. Diffusers and sunny glass give false readings."),
("For an outdoor reset control, why is the outdoor sensor mounted on a north wall?",
 "Shaded from the sun", ["Closest to the boiler", "Away from rain", "Easiest to wire"],
 "Sun on the sensor reads warmer than the true air temperature and lowers the water temperature too much. A shaded north wall gives a stable reading."),
("What is listed in a refrigeration material take-off?",
 "Pipe and fittings by size", ["Hourly labour rates by trade", "Refrigerant prices", "Client contact details"],
 "A take-off counts the materials needed from the drawings: tubing lengths, fittings, valves, hangers and insulation by size, so the job can be ordered and planned."),
("Before planning a retrofit, why is the existing system's energy use measured?",
 "To set a baseline for savings", ["To size the new electrical panel", "To find its refrigerant type", "To meet the warranty terms"],
 "A retrofit plan compares current energy use with the expected use after the change. Without a baseline, savings and payback cannot be estimated."),
("A building's ducts were sized for heating only. What must be checked before adding central cooling?",
 "Duct size for cooling", ["Chimney liner condition", "Boiler water pressure", "Gas meter size"],
 "Cooling usually needs more airflow than heating. Undersized ducts cause low airflow, frozen coils and noise, so the distribution is assessed in the retrofit plan."),
("An R-22 system is being converted to a replacement refrigerant. What does the plan check first?",
 "Approved refrigerant and oil", ["Colour of the new cylinder", "Length of the electrical run", "Size of the condensate drain line"],
 "Retrofits follow the compressor and refrigerant makers' guidance, since oil type, seals, expansion devices and capacity can all change with the new refrigerant."),
("Why may an oversized replacement air conditioner leave a home feeling clammy?",
 "It short cycles", ["It runs too long each cycle", "It pulls in too much outdoor air", "It overcharges the refrigerant"],
 "An oversized unit satisfies the thermostat quickly, so the coil is not cold long enough to wring out moisture. Right-sizing improves comfort and efficiency."),
("Which device recovers both heat and moisture from exhaust air in winter?",
 "Energy recovery ventilator", ["Heat recovery ventilator", "Exhaust fan with a backdraft damper", "Electronic air cleaner"],
 "An ERV core transfers sensible heat and moisture between the air streams. An HRV transfers heat only."),
("Why is an economizer useful on a rooftop unit in an office building in spring?",
 "Free cooling from outdoor air", ["It heats the outdoor air", "It dehumidifies with the burner", "It lowers the fan speed"],
 "When outdoor air is cool and dry enough, the economizer opens outdoor dampers to cool the space with outside air, so the compressor runs less."),
("What design value from a refrigerant line set chart limits suction line pressure drop?",
 "Equivalent length", ["Wall thickness alone", "Insulation colour", "Tube hardness"],
 "Line sizing uses equivalent length: the straight length plus an allowance for each fitting. Longer equivalent lengths need larger tubing to keep pressure drop in range."),
("Why is the refrigerant charge recalculated when a split system's line set is longer than standard?",
 "Longer liquid line holds more", ["Longer lines raise the voltage", "The oil changes type", "The compressor speeds up"],
 "Factory charges cover a set line length. Additional length holds more liquid in the liquid line, so the maker gives extra charge per metre."),
]
